00:19:59.200 --> 00:20:04.880
compartment, millions of little compartments&nbsp;
in them. And they're completely passive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.880 --> 00:20:13.360
They're enclosed in a vacuum and they only expand&nbsp;
because there are muscles pulling on the tissues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:13.360 --> 00:20:21.200
next to them. So the muscles we use to breathe are&nbsp;
called the diaphragm. And the diaphragm is a dome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:21.200 --> 00:20:30.560
shaped muscle that sits over your abdominal organs&nbsp;
just like this. And when that muscle contracts, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.560 --> 00:20:39.600
flattens out and it pushes the abdominal contents&nbsp;
downward. So when you breathe properly, your belly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:39.600 --> 00:20:47.120
is supposed to come out. That's supposed to&nbsp;
happen. Now, unfortunately, so many people are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:47.120 --> 00:20:55.840
concerned about their flat stomach that they force&nbsp;
their stomach in when the diaphragm is pushing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:55.840 --> 00:21:00.880
down. and now they're kind of short circuiting&nbsp;
the whole system and they're using other muscles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.880 --> 00:21:07.680
and creating stress. The second important muscle&nbsp;
for breathing is called your intercostal muscles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:15.440
and costal that's for ribs. That's another name&nbsp;
for your ribs. So it's muscles in between your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:15.440 --> 00:21:23.840
ribs. And one way to visualize this is that these&nbsp;
muscles, the ribs are like bucket handles. They're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:23.840 --> 00:21:31.040
hanging down off the side of the bucket. And if&nbsp;
you were to raise those handles just a little bit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:31.040 --> 00:21:40.320
then you expand the whole rib cage just a little&nbsp;
bit and it's moving up and out ever so slightly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.320 --> 00:21:47.200
So that's what the intercostal muscles do. And&nbsp;
then there is one more muscle group and those are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:47.200 --> 00:21:52.080
the accessory muscles and I put them down below&nbsp;
because they're like a different story. We'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.080 --> 00:22:00.480
get to that. the accessory muscles, they pull from&nbsp;
the top of the rib cage and from your collar bone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.480 --> 00:22:07.520
and up. And that's not a normal way of breathing.&nbsp;
That's not what you want to do when you're doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:07.520 --> 00:22:13.680
these types of breaths. So, we'll go through this&nbsp;
in detail so you know exactly how this works.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.680 --> 00:22:21.600
So for the diaphragm, the portion where your&nbsp;
diaphragm is pushing down and your abdominal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.600 --> 00:22:32.080
contents are being pushed out, you count to three.&nbsp;
So that first portion is one, two, three. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:32.080 --> 00:22:41.360
during that time only the abdominal area should&nbsp;
move. Your belly should come out. And then if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:41.360 --> 00:22:48.400
wanted to keep going with only the diaphragm, it&nbsp;
would be strained. So then the intercostal muscles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:48.400 --> 00:22:57.120
kick in. So when you go to a count of four and&nbsp;
five, now your rib cage expands ever so slightly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:57.120 --> 00:23:04.160
and it's going to lift up just a little bit, but&nbsp;
it's not going to involve the shoulders. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:04.160 --> 00:23:12.160
after that slight expansion of the rib cage, now&nbsp;
you just let it rest for just you could rest for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:12.160 --> 00:23:18.480
a second, maybe two seconds, or just a fraction of&nbsp;
a second. It doesn't matter. But there's a point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:18.480 --> 00:23:28.800
where you're done with the breath where nothing's&nbsp;
happening. So you breathe in one, two, three,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:28.800 --> 00:23:35.520
four, five, six, and it just sits there. Okay. And&nbsp;
we'll go through this a few times so you really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:35.520 --> 00:23:45.040
get it. And you can try this as we go. And then&nbsp;
you want to focus on your breath being very slow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:45.040 --> 00:23:52.880
It should be completely silent. It should be so&nbsp;
slow that you make no noise. You should not hear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:52.880 --> 00:24:00.640
it because then you're breathing too much air.&nbsp;
This is also not what we would call a deep breath.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:01.200 --> 00:24:07.440
It could be a tiny bit deeper than a shallow&nbsp;
breath like your normal shallow breath. Just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:07.440 --> 00:24:14.320
go. That's shallow. You can go a little bit&nbsp;
deeper, but it's not a deep breath where you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:14.320 --> 00:24:19.920
fill your lungs because now you're kicking in&nbsp;
these accessory muscles and it becomes more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:19.920 --> 00:24:28.240
of a forced breath. And then obviously after that&nbsp;
you breathe out. And this is a completely passive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:28.240 --> 00:24:35.120
process. That's for a count of 1 through six.&nbsp;
And you take a rest at the bottom when when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:35.120 --> 00:24:42.160
air has left. Then there's a moment where you&nbsp;
have a very brief rest. And this exhalation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:42.160 --> 00:24:48.480
the breathing out is passive. It's just&nbsp;
the elastic recoil. So when you breathe in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:48.480 --> 00:24:53.120
even though it's very gentle, you're you're&nbsp;
tightening up some muscles in your diaphragm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:53.120 --> 00:24:58.560
and your rib cage. And when you breathe out, you&nbsp;
just let them relax and they're going to return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:58.560 --> 00:25:04.800
to their original shape. And these accessory&nbsp;
muscles, they're important, but not for breathing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:04.800 --> 00:25:12.640
exercises. They are for doing deep forced breaths,&nbsp;
like in an emergency or if you take a super quick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:12.640 --> 00:25:19.920
if you sniff in like then that's like an emergency&nbsp;
breath. You see your like the muscles, the tendons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:19.920 --> 00:25:26.240
on the front of your neck kick in because those&nbsp;
muscles are pulling the rib cage and the collar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:26.240 --> 00:25:33.280
bone upwards. Now, you don't want to do that at&nbsp;
all during this type of breathing because that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:33.280 --> 00:25:38.640
is tied to a stress response. When you have an&nbsp;
emergency, that's a stress response. You're trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:38.640 --> 00:25:47.280
to get as much air in as fast as possible, but&nbsp;
that is very counterproductive to what we do here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:47.280 --> 00:25:54.000
And for the people who are supposed to breathe&nbsp;
from their diaphragm, but they're concerned with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:54.000 --> 00:26:01.440
their belly going out, so they hold their stomach&nbsp;
in, what usually happens is they switch to using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:01.440 --> 00:26:10.560
their accessory muscles instead and they breathe&nbsp;
like this, which is not very relaxing at all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:11.120 --> 00:26:18.880
And another important thing is when I say the slow&nbsp;
and silent and not too deep. A lot of people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:18.880 --> 00:26:25.440
try to do this, they breathe too fast, but they&nbsp;
know they're supposed to keep the in breath for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:25.440 --> 00:26:32.240
five seconds. So they just overfill their lungs&nbsp;
and very often they get dizzy and lightheaded and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:32.240 --> 00:26:38.880
they say, "Oh, this doesn't work. I get dizzy."&nbsp;
No, you're breathing too much. you're taking in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:38.880 --> 00:26:48.000
way too much air. That's why I really emphasize&nbsp;
the slow, silent, and just maybe 20, maybe 50%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:48.000 --> 00:26:56.320
deeper than a shallow breath. But it should be&nbsp;
very, very relaxed and quiet and silent. So, let&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:56.320 --> 00:27:04.080
me show you one more way that you can think about&nbsp;
this. And if this represents a breath, then at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:04.080 --> 00:27:11.200
at the very beginning, which would be at the end&nbsp;
of your last outbreath, there's a slight pause.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:11.200 --> 00:27:16.560
It's just a period where nothing is happening.&nbsp;
You're not breathing in, you're not breathing out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:16.560 --> 00:27:22.240
and then all of a sudden the breath just kind&nbsp;
of starts by itself. It's like here, nothing's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:22.240 --> 00:27:30.000
happening. And all of a sudden, you notice, hey,&nbsp;
air is coming in. And then it goes up. And now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:41.200
one, two, three is the only the belly moving. So&nbsp;
I'm going to be quiet and just kind of show you.

00:27:41.200 --> 00:27:50.320
So that's the one, two, three. And then we have&nbsp;
the four and five, which looks like your rib&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:50.320 --> 00:27:57.840
cage just expands a little bit very gently,&nbsp;
very relaxed. And then right at the top here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:57.840 --> 00:28:03.680
is again a period where you're not breathing&nbsp;
in, you're not breathing out. There's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:03.680 --> 00:28:10.720
nothing happening. And all of a sudden, maybe&nbsp;
it's half a second, maybe it's two seconds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:10.720 --> 00:28:18.560
you're finding yourself breathing out again.&nbsp;
And you just very, very gently and slowly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:18.560 --> 00:28:25.120
passively release that air. And then at the end&nbsp;
here, which is the beginning of the next breath,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:25.120 --> 00:28:31.360
there's a period where nothing's happening. And&nbsp;
then all of a sudden, you're breathing in again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:31.360 --> 00:28:37.600
So, I know this is a lot of technical explanation,&nbsp;
but it's just to try to help you understand how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:37.600 --> 00:28:43.120
it's going to work. Obviously, when you're doing&nbsp;
it later, and the more you do it, the more this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:43.120 --> 00:28:48.480
is going to run on automatic. And once you have&nbsp;
the mechanics down, there's this little thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:48.480 --> 00:28:56.640
called emotion that can act as a multiplier to the&nbsp;
relaxation effects and to the benefit. So once you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:56.640 --> 00:29:07.200
have the mechanics down, now when you breathe in,&nbsp;
you visualize breathing in life or health or joy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:07.200 --> 00:29:14.800
or anything else that works for you, that makes&nbsp;
you feel really good, that makes sense to you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:14.800 --> 00:29:23.680
And then as you breathe out now you visualize&nbsp;
breathing out stress or tension or problems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:23.680 --> 00:29:32.560
You could tell yourself in this moment everything&nbsp;
is absolutely okay. I can put everything away just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:32.560 --> 00:29:38.400
for this moment. I can start worrying again in&nbsp;
a minute. That's all right. I know I have these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:38.400 --> 00:29:44.800
issues I got to deal with, but for right now,&nbsp;
I'm allowing myself not to bother with that. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:44.800 --> 00:29:51.840
could also visualize putting down your burdens.&nbsp;
Like life very often feels like a burden and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:51.840 --> 00:29:57.520
can imagine that you're carrying it all on your&nbsp;
shoulders and then on the outbreath you're just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:57.520 --> 00:30:06.160
allowing yourself to put it down just for now. And&nbsp;
usually when you do that, you can feel yourself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:06.160 --> 00:30:12.240
sort of getting an inch taller instantaneously.&nbsp;
It's such a wonderful relief. You can also during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:12.240 --> 00:30:21.040
this whole process, breathing in or out or both,&nbsp;
you could just silently say the word calm to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:21.040 --> 00:30:31.280
yourself or say the word peace to yourself. Other&nbsp;
words you could try is happy or content or bliss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:31.280 --> 00:30:39.280
which means that you just feel good for absolutely&nbsp;
no reason other than that you're feeling good. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:39.280 --> 00:30:44.560
this is very different from how we're typically&nbsp;
conditioned because we're always told that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:44.560 --> 00:30:51.200
should strive, we should work hard, we only&nbsp;
deserve to feel good when we have accomplished&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:51.200 --> 00:30:58.560
something. So first you work really hard, you feel&nbsp;
really bad, you overcome something and then you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:58.560 --> 00:31:06.400
accomplish a goal and then you're allowed to feel&nbsp;
happy without guilt. But the problem with that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:06.400 --> 00:31:10.640
that once you have accomplished this thing,&nbsp;
there's another thing and another thing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:10.640 --> 00:31:16.640
another thing. So if you don't allow yourself&nbsp;
to feel good during the process, then you will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:16.640 --> 00:31:23.680
never arrive at that happiness. Happiness is not&nbsp;
something that you get to. Feeling good is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:23.680 --> 00:31:29.680
something you get to. It's something that you are.&nbsp;
It's something that you allow yourself to be. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:29.680 --> 00:31:37.040
you want to change this accomplish in order to&nbsp;
be happy. And you want to change that to happy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:37.040 --> 00:31:44.320
in order to accomplish. So once you do these&nbsp;
exercises and you do this breathing exercises,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:44.320 --> 00:31:50.560
whether it's for 30 seconds or five minutes, and&nbsp;
you're going to notice with just a little bit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:50.560 --> 00:31:57.120
practice how incredibly effective this is, not&nbsp;
just in lowering blood pressure, but in just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:57.120 --> 00:32:04.240
feeling good. And you allow yourself to take that&nbsp;
feeling with you. You will notice that you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:04.240 --> 00:32:10.240
still get things done. You can still accomplish&nbsp;
things. In fact, you do them even better. But the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:10.240 --> 00:32:16.160
biggest difference is you're feeling good as you&nbsp;
do them. So, I would encourage you either now or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:16.160 --> 00:32:24.800
later to bookmark this and then you replay the&nbsp;
last two slides and you practice the breath as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:24.800 --> 00:32:31.760
I described it and you practice with these words&nbsp;
and you practice with these visualizations and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:31.760 --> 00:32:36.720
scenarios. And I think you'll find in just&nbsp;
a couple of breaths, sometimes that even the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:36.720 --> 00:32:43.120
first breath, you notice how you feel completely&nbsp;
different. So what actually happens now that we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:43.120 --> 00:32:49.120
we've learned how to do it, you may have practiced&nbsp;
and you're feeling good, but what actually happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:49.120 --> 00:32:54.880
in the body? Well, short-term and long-term,&nbsp;
there's some profound differences. So short-term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:54.880 --> 00:33:02.080
the very first thing and dramatically important&nbsp;
is a pattern interrupt. When we feel stressed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:02.080 --> 00:33:07.280
when we feel tense, when we have high blood&nbsp;
pressure, it's because we're in a certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:07.280 --> 00:33:16.880
state. And that state is created by our thoughts,&nbsp;
which usually run in an automatic loop. And the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:16.880 --> 00:33:22.960
first thing that your breath, your slow breath&nbsp;
accomplishes is to interrupt that pattern. You're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:22.960 --> 00:33:30.000
just doing something different. That thought loop&nbsp;
cannot continue when you interrupt it. And next,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:36.640
of course, I mentioned that you're going to go&nbsp;
from firing your vagus nerve, your parasympathetic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:36.640 --> 00:33:43.600
nervous system, your relaxation response. You go&nbsp;
from firing that for about 1 second up to about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:43.600 --> 00:33:51.360
5 seconds. You're allowing it time to kick in.&nbsp;
And as a result, your heart rate will go down and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:51.360 --> 00:33:57.760
your blood pressure will go down. For some people,&nbsp;
it's going to happen within seconds or a minute or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:57.760 --> 00:34:06.800
three. And for other people, they kind of have&nbsp;
a lot of "stuckage". Their their physiology is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:06.800 --> 00:34:13.840
just not that flexible yet. And it could take&nbsp;
practicing this for a few weeks, but it doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:13.840 --> 00:34:21.600
mean that it's not working. It just means that&nbsp;
you have to fire more parasympathetic for a longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:21.600 --> 00:34:26.880
period of time. It's like you're chipping away at&nbsp;
something and it's going to take a while before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:26.880 --> 00:34:34.800
it gives. You're going to stabilize your heart&nbsp;
rhythms. When they do EKGs and when they measure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:34.800 --> 00:34:41.120
your heart function, they measure how steady&nbsp;
the rhythm is and they measure your arrhythmias,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:41.120 --> 00:34:47.120
the irregularities, and you're going to find&nbsp;
that all of that gets better. What also happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:47.120 --> 00:34:56.320
is vasodilation. You're going to relax your blood&nbsp;
vessels, which results in warmer hands and feet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:56.320 --> 00:35:03.600
So, if you're suffering from cold hands and feet,&nbsp;
then these exercises are probably going to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:03.600 --> 00:35:10.000
care of that as well pretty dramatically. And&nbsp;
again, it's not going to work if you're sitting in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:10.000 --> 00:35:15.600
an ice cold air conditioning rooms where the vent&nbsp;
is blasting on you. You're not going to overcome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:15.600 --> 00:35:21.600
that. But if you're sitting in a comfortable&nbsp;
room and you still have cold hands and feet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:21.600 --> 00:35:28.320
then that's not a good thing. that that's a&nbsp;
stress response. That's the vasoconstriction of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:28.320 --> 00:35:34.400
stress keeping the blood from flowing through your&nbsp;
hands and feet. You will also notice a decrease&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:34.400 --> 00:35:41.760
of sweating of the palm of your hand. And for&nbsp;
some people, this is not a big deal. For others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:41.760 --> 00:35:49.280
it's devastating. It can ruin their life. They&nbsp;
can't touch anything because they leave wet spots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:49.280 --> 00:35:55.680
everywhere. If they put a hand on a shirt, it&nbsp;
leaves a big hand imprint. It can be that bad.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:55.680 --> 00:36:00.400
Some people, they never want to shake hands&nbsp;
with anybody because it's so embarrassing. Or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:00.400 --> 00:36:06.960
you see people wiping their hands on their pants&nbsp;
before they they reach out to greet you. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:06.960 --> 00:36:15.360
that's excessive sweating and it's driven by your&nbsp;
stress response and it can be dramatically reduced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:15.360 --> 00:36:23.600
by these exercises. And you will also notice an&nbsp;
increase in your ability to focus and stay calm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:23.600 --> 00:36:31.840
even under stress because you're training your&nbsp;
body to reduce the stress response. And the stress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:31.840 --> 00:36:39.120
response is going to allocate resources for you&nbsp;
to run away. It's going to take blood away from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:39.120 --> 00:36:46.720
your focus center, the frontal lobe. So, as you&nbsp;
relax, as you increase your relaxation response,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:46.720 --> 00:36:54.400
as you warm your hands and feet, you're also going&nbsp;
to get more blood back to your frontal brain and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:54.400 --> 00:37:01.280
increase your focus and your calm. And I'll never&nbsp;
forget a teenage kid I had as a patient. He came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:01.280 --> 00:37:08.400
in for back pain or some musculoskeletal issue,&nbsp;
but he had all these things I was talking about.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:08.400 --> 00:37:14.960
He wouldn't shake hands. He would wipe his hands.&nbsp;
He was a super smart kid, but he did so poorly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:14.960 --> 00:37:23.840
on tests. He was getting B's and C's at best.&nbsp;
And he was afraid of meeting girls because his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:23.840 --> 00:37:32.480
handers were so sweaty. He was nervous all the&nbsp;
time. So I had him work on these exercises and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:32.480 --> 00:37:41.840
within weeks his sweating was down dramatically.&nbsp;
Within a couple of months, he went from a B or C&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:41.840 --> 00:37:49.040
student to straight A's on all his tests, and he&nbsp;
was so calm and feeling so much better instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:49.040 --> 00:37:53.840
having anxiety. And again, everyone's different.&nbsp;
For some people, you're going to see changes like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:53.840 --> 00:37:59.440
that in a matter of weeks. And for others, it&nbsp;
could take a year. You could have a whole lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:59.440 --> 00:38:06.800
of chipping away to do, but it's still worth doing&nbsp;
it because you're changing things for the better.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:06.800 --> 00:38:11.840
Even things you don't notice, you're going to&nbsp;
be improving them in the process. And long term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:11.840 --> 00:38:18.800
it gets really, really exciting because there's&nbsp;
something called neuroplasticity. Most people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:18.800 --> 00:38:24.000
especially the ones who don't watch this whole&nbsp;
video, they're just looking for that 30- second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:24.000 --> 00:38:29.840
tidbit. They're going to try this and they go, "Oh&nbsp;
yeah, I felt better, but it didn't last." Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:29.840 --> 00:38:36.240
it's because you didn't do it again and again and&nbsp;
again. And that's where neuroplasticity comes in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:36.240 --> 00:38:43.440
Neuroplasticity is the ability of your nervous&nbsp;
system to change, to physically change shape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:43.440 --> 00:38:52.640
to rewire itself, to repattern and retrain.&nbsp;
Whenever you learn a new skill, then you're making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:52.640 --> 00:38:59.440
new synapses. You're making new connection. Your&nbsp;
nervous system is rewiring itself. And staying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:59.440 --> 00:39:05.840
calm and breathing different is a skill. That's&nbsp;
a state in your nervous system. That's a skill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:05.840 --> 00:39:12.000
and you can learn it. And in doing this, you're&nbsp;
learning the skill of calm. You're learning the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:12.000 --> 00:39:20.720
skill of better focus. But the parasympathetic and&nbsp;
the vagus nerve, it does handle blood pressure. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:20.720 --> 00:39:27.440
will help you lower blood pressure, but it does so&nbsp;
much more. It handles the supply to all your vital&nbsp;&nbsp;

